A term-rewriting system (more generally, a rewriting system with bound variables, such as a combinatory reduction system) is given by a set of function symbols (each with a fixed arity) together with a set of rewrite rules. The system is called:
- finitely generated if it has finitely many function symbols and finitely many rewrite rules;
- full if its set of terms consists of all terms that can be built from its function symbols (i.e. there is no restriction to a subset such as well-typed terms);
- Church-Rosser (confluent) if whenever a term t reduces to terms u and v, there exists a term w to which both u and v reduce.

A one-step reduction strategy is a function F that assigns to every term t not in normal form a single redex F(t) inside t (equivalently, a term F(t) obtained from t by contracting one redex). The strategy is recursive (computable) if F is a computable function. It is normalizing if, for every term t that has a normal form, the sequence t, F(t), F(F(t)), … eventually reaches a normal form.

Question: Must every full, finitely generated, Church-Rosser term-rewriting system (or system with bound variables) admit a recursive one-step normalizing reduction strategy?

